# Target C-II — a linear high-`t` shore has a one-bar / exact-antipodal obstruction fork

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proof-only high-`t` structural strengthening; corrected antipodal split  
**Scope:** canonical full-core/no-macroscopic-container Type-II branch with `t=(q-k)/D<=1.8687`  
**Dependencies:** high-`t` complete rectangle; rectangle orientation/phase theorem; global finite-defect/KKT/angular budget; common diameter-routing hub; common-transversal/rooted-shore sign; one/two-bar tangent theorem; non-antipodal minimal two-bar geometry; adjacent-strip reaction nondegeneracy; exact-antipodal second-order blocker theorem; canonical C67/DB.3 diameter-direction separation  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_high_t_linear_one_bar_cusp_density_t18687.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The full high-`t` rectangle contains a linear consecutive row shore. On one side of the unique diameter-routing hub all switching sets are one-sided and pairwise support-disjoint. Spending the global defect/KKT/angular budget directly on that shore gives a linear family of uniformly bounded interfaces.

There is one necessary distinction. The existing minimal two-bar localization theorem applies only when the two rooted boundary vectors are **not exactly antipodal**. In that non-antipodal branch, genuine two-bar blockers are globally sublinear by inverse-`D` diameter-direction separation. Exact-antipodal strips have a different second-order obstruction: they require switching diameters of opposite transverse sign and cannot be folded into the near-parallel two-bar packing argument.

The correct conclusion is therefore a sharp fork:

\[
\boxed{
|E_D|+|U_D|>0.00000565D-o(D),
}
\]

where `E_D` is the exact-antipodal good-interface set and `U_D` is the non-antipodal one-bar good-interface set. The remaining non-antipodal genuine two-bar set is `o(D)`.

---

## 1. A linear one-sided shore of the rectangle

Let

\[
A_*=(\tau_1,\ldots,\tau_m)
\]

be the consecutive row block from the high-`t` complete rectangle. The rectangle theorem gives

\[
\boxed{
m>cD-o(D),
\qquad
c:=0.0000226={113\over5{,}000{,}000}.
}
\tag{OF.1}
\]

The common-hub theorem supplies one hub boundary `k`. Delete that boundary and the fixed `O(1)` end indices required by the adjacent-strip setup. Of the two remaining sides, choose the one containing more internal row interfaces and call its interface set `I_D`. Then

\[
\boxed{
|I_D|>{c\over2}D-o(D)
=0.0000113D-o(D).
}
\tag{OF.2}
\]

Orient this side toward the hub. The one-sided switching theorem says every actual diameter interval that switches on this side has the same start/stop orientation. Consequently one physical diameter edge can switch at at most one interface of `I_D`. Hence

\[
\boxed{
H_{sw,i}\cap H_{sw,j}=\varnothing
\qquad(i\ne j,\ i,j\in I_D).
}
\tag{OF.3}
\]

This disjointness will make direction packing global rather than interface-by-interface.

---

## 2. A linear number of uniformly bounded interfaces

Use the joint-cusp cost

\[
J_i:=N_i+L_i+D B_i+S_i+S_{i+1}\ge0.
\tag{OF.4}
\]

Here `N_i` is switching actual-diameter support size, `L_i` is the assigned terminal face-side count, `B_i` is switching KKT multiplier mass, and `S_j` is the row angular-defect sum against the common oriented column family `B_+`.

The audited global budgets used in the fixed-chain theorem give

\[
\sum_iJ_i<KD+o(D),
\tag{OF.5}
\]

where

\[
K=10T-2\pi+2+2C_{phase},
\qquad
T=1.8687,
\]

and the exact certificate proves the strict margin

\[
\boxed{K<Cc,
\qquad
C:=1{,}109{,}000.}
\tag{OF.6}
\]

Call `i in I_D` **linear-good** when

\[
J_i<4C=4{,}436{,}000.
\tag{OF.7}
\]

If `b_D` interfaces of `I_D` are bad, then

\[
4C b_D\le\sum_iJ_i<CcD+o(D),
\]

so

\[
b_D<{c\over4}D+o(D).
\]

Combining this with (OF.2), the good set `G_D` satisfies

\[
\boxed{
|G_D|>{c\over4}D-o(D)
=0.00000565D-o(D).
}
\tag{OF.8}
\]

For every `i in G_D`, nonnegativity in (OF.4) gives

\[
\boxed{
N_i,L_i<4{,}436{,}000,
\qquad
B_i<{4{,}436{,}000\over D},
\qquad
S_i+S_{i+1}<4{,}436{,}000.
}
\tag{OF.9}
\]

Thus the rectangle contains a genuine `Theta(D)` family of bounded-complexity, one-sided local obstruction interfaces.

---

## 3. Every non-antipodal good strip has inverse-diameter opening width

The rectangle orientation theorem supplies one common column family

\[
B_+,
\qquad
|B_+|>0.1567D-o(D),
\tag{OF.10}
\]

and consistent real row phases `alpha_i`. As in the joint-cusp theorem,

\[
|B_+|\,|\alpha_{i+1}-\alpha_i|
\le S_i+S_{i+1}.
\tag{OF.11}
\]

Therefore every good interface obeys

\[
|\alpha_{i+1}-\alpha_i|
<{4C\over0.1567D-o(D)}.
\]

The certificate checks

\[
{4C\over0.1567}<2.831\times10^7.
\]

For a non-antipodal interface, the rooted-shore sign theorem identifies the positive real phase gap with the adjacent-strip opening width

\[
\varepsilon_i
:=\pi-\angle(a_i,b_i)>0.
\]

Hence

\[
\boxed{
\varepsilon_i
<{2.831\times10^7\over D}+o(D^{-1}).
}
\tag{OF.12}
\]

For an exact-antipodal interface `b_i=-a_i`, one has `epsilon_i=0`; that case is separated below rather than passed through the non-antipodal two-bar theorem.

---

## 4. Correct local partition

Partition the good interfaces into three disjoint classes.

### Exact-antipodal class `E_D`

These are the interfaces with

\[
\boxed{b_i=-a_i.}
\tag{OF.13}
\]

The exact layer reaction is

\[
G_i=A_i a_i+B_i^{ct}b_i,
\tag{OF.14}
\]

where `A_i,B_i^{ct}>0` are the two boundary contact-force totals. Reaction nondegeneracy gives `G_i!=0`, so in this branch

\[
\boxed{A_i\ne B_i^{ct},
\qquad
G_i=(A_i-B_i^{ct})a_i\ne0.}
\tag{OF.15}
\]

On the other hand the exact-antipodal second-order blocker theorem says that every canonical survivor contains two switching diameter edges with opposite transverse signs relative to `a_i`.

Because the switching reaction is the positive sum of the switching KKT forces,

\[
|G_i|
\le D\sum_{h\in H_{sw,i}}\beta_h
=D B_i
<4{,}436{,}000.
\tag{OF.16}
\]

Thus every exact-antipodal good interface has a **nonzero but uniformly bounded axial reaction** and an opposite-transverse switching pair.

### Non-antipodal one-bar class `U_D`

These are the interfaces with `b_i!=-a_i` for which some single switching diameter edge is already a tangent certificate.

### Non-antipodal genuine two-bar class `T_D`

These are the remaining non-antipodal interfaces. By the one/two-bar tangent theorem, a minimal tangent certificate has exactly two switching actual diameter bars, and neither bar alone is a certificate.

The three classes exhaust `G_D`:

\[
\boxed{G_D=E_D\sqcup U_D\sqcup T_D.}
\tag{OF.17}
\]

---

## 5. Only the non-antipodal genuine two-bar class is direction-localized

Fix `i in T_D`. Rotate coordinates so

\[
a_i=(1,0),
\qquad
b_i=(\cos(\pi-\varepsilon_i),\sin(\pi-\varepsilon_i)).
\]

The proof of the existing minimal two-bar geometry theorem parameterizes the two blocker directions by

\[
\phi_1=s_1+{\pi\over2},
\qquad
\phi_2=s_2-{\pi\over2},
\qquad
0\le s_1\le s_2\le\varepsilon_i.
\]

After projectivizing the second direction, each actual diameter direction lies within `epsilon_i` of the projective normal to `a_i`:

\[
\boxed{
 d_{\mathbb{RP}^1}([n_{i,j}],[a_i^\perp])
\le\varepsilon_i,
\qquad j=1,2.}
\tag{OF.18}
\]

This statement is deliberately restricted to `T_D`; it is not asserted for exact-antipodal interfaces.

---

## 6. The rectangle phase window has room for only `o(D)` non-antipodal TB2 interfaces

All rows of `A_*` come from one shrinking dominant projective phase window. Write its width as

\[
\omega_D=o(1)
\tag{OF.19}
\]

about a projective direction `phi_D`.

Equations (OF.12), (OF.18), and (OF.19) imply that every actual diameter bar selected in a `T_D` certificate has projective direction inside one common ball around `phi_D^perp` of radius

\[
R_D
:=\omega_D+{2.831\times10^7\over D}+o(D^{-1})
=o(1).
\tag{OF.20}
\]

Canonical C67/DB.3 says distinct actual diameter edges have distinct projective directions separated by

\[
\delta_D=2\arcsin{1\over2D}\ge {1\over D}.
\tag{OF.21}
\]

A projective ball of radius `R_D` is an arc of length at most `2R_D`, so it contains at most

\[
1+{2R_D\over\delta_D}
\le1+2DR_D
=o(D)
\tag{OF.22}
\]

distinct actual diameter directions.

Every interface in `T_D` contributes two distinct certificate bars. By (OF.3), certificate bars from different interfaces are also distinct. Therefore

\[
2|T_D|\le1+2DR_D=o(D),
\]

and hence

\[
\boxed{|T_D|=o(D).}
\tag{OF.23}
\]

This is the valid global packing conclusion: **non-antipodal** genuine two-bar blockers cannot occur at linear density.

---

## 7. The linear obstruction fork

Use (OF.8), (OF.17), and (OF.23):

\[
|E_D|+|U_D|
=|G_D|-|T_D|
>0.00000565D-o(D).
\]

Thus

\[
\boxed{
|E_D|+|U_D|>0.00000565D-o(D).
}
\tag{OF.24}
\]

In particular

\[
\boxed{
\max\{|E_D|,|U_D|\}
>0.000002825D-o(D).
}
\tag{OF.25}
\]

The surviving high-`t` geometry must therefore carry a linear-density instance of at least one of two highly specific mechanisms:

```text
EA branch:
    exact antipodal rooted boundary vectors b_i=-a_i;
    nonzero axial reaction G_i=(A_i-B_i^ct)a_i;
    |G_i|<4,436,000 on every good interface;
    an opposite-transverse switching-diameter pair;
    one-sided switching toward the same global hub.

TB1 branch:
    one switching actual diameter bar alone blocks every local tangent opening;
    bars chosen at different interfaces are distinct;
    all switches are one-sided toward the same global hub.
```

This is stronger than the earlier growing diagonal chain because it holds at positive linear density on the full rectangle shore.

---

## 8. What remains

The non-antipodal two-bar branch is now globally negligible. Closure can focus on two alternatives.

1. **Exact-antipodal density:** exploit a linear ordered family of exact antipodal row pairs with nonzero bounded axial reactions and opposite-transverse switching pairs. The missing step is a global order/variation theorem ruling out or exploiting so many exact axial reactions on one hub side.
2. **One-bar density:** exploit a linear ordered family of single-bar tangent blockers together with their distinct switching positions, the convex diameter-thrackle order, and the common linear near-triangular transverse ladder.

Either target is substantially more rigid than an arbitrary bounded local cusp.

---

## 9. Scope audit

- The linear-good extraction uses the whole rectangle budget directly and does not claim that total switching KKT mass over the whole linear shore is `o(1)`.
- Switching-support disjointness is used only after choosing one side of the common hub.
- The inverse-diameter opening estimate (OF.12) is used for non-antipodal strips. Exact antipodality is treated separately.
- The minimal two-bar localization theorem is **not** applied to `b_i=-a_i`; this is the correction relative to the first version of this note.
- Exact-antipodal reaction nondegeneracy gives `A_i!=B_i^ct`, but no lower bound on `|A_i-B_i^ct|` is claimed.
- A one-bar certificate need not point near `phi_D^perp`; no such localization is used here.
- No individual KKT multiplier lower bound is assumed or proved.
- No C-II or Level-I closure is claimed.

**Disposition:** proved a linear family of uniformly bounded one-sided high-`t` interfaces, proved that only `o(D)` non-antipodal interfaces can remain genuine two-bar blockers, and reduced the linear-density survivor to an exact-antipodal / one-bar fork. C-II and Level I remain open.
